EVT2730 TEXT x.MainFrame.ThermalConsFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel <b>Coolant Pumps</b> or C-Pumps <b>degrade at a constant rate</b> due to the corrosive nature of ISE, and this is made worse <b>based on</b> the <b>pump level.</b> It's recommended you <b>observe the reservior level and reactor chamber Coolant Pipes</b> to guage how close it is to failing.
EVT2731 TEXT x.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.ThermalConsFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel When the core is in an <b>Equinox State, pressure systems</b> have been noted to be <b>ineffective</b> due to an as of yet unkown phenomena that affects several systems and components in the installation.
EVT2732 TEXT x.DRMScreen.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.ThermalConsFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel Additionally, the <b>C-Pumps</b> have been observed <b>degrading twice as fast</b> as their usual rate, which at times <b>ruptures or completely destroys</b> the reactor's <b>coolant</b> network.
EVT2733 TEXT x.OperationsFrame.TLDRLabel TLDR: This console controls outtake fans and C-Pumps which help reduce the core's pressure and temperature. Higher coolant levels will cause them to degrade faster, necessitating more repairs.
EVT2734 TEXT x.ElectricGridConsole.DRMScreen.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.ThermalConsFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel Should the <b>pumps fail,</b> you are required to go in the <b>heavy maintenance sub-sector,</b> and make the appropriate <b>repairs / recallibrations.</b>
EVT2735 TEXT x.MainFrame.TextLabel This monitor is responsible for displaying all vital information about both the subspace core and some of the major components of the reactor chamber.<br /><b>[CBL's, Coolant, P.E.A, H.D.E.F, Pressure, Radiation, Temperature]</b>
EVT2736 TEXT x.MonitorsFrame.PowerFrame.TextLabel Each CBL has their own <b>respective diagnostics</b> displayed in a simplified format, with the most <b>notable</b> being <b>Stress & Pressure.</b>
EVT2737 TEXT x.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.MonitorsFrame.PowerFrame.TextLabel <b>Refer to the [LASER CONS] Panel for more information</b> regarding CBL Statuses, and how to handle them.
EVT2738 TEXT x.CoolantFrame.TextLabel This monitor is responsible for displaying information about both the <b>coolant and chamber fan statuses.<br />[C-PUMP, OUTTAKE FAN]</b>
EVT2739 TEXT x.MonitorsFrame.CoolantFrame.TextLabel The appropriate C-Pump will be displayed in red for repairs, however <u><b>coolant sensory equipment has a tendency to be unreliable</b></u> during reactor opration.
EVT2740 TEXT x.MainFrame.MonitorsFrame.CoolantFrame.TextLabel When a coolant pump's <b>net stability value is low,</b> it will appear be <b>leaking</b> within the chamber, and when its <b>almost fully depleted,</b> its <b>reservior value will appear to decrease.</b> 
EVT2741 TEXT x.AlertsFrame.TextLabel This monitor displays information regarding alerts and notable information about the reactor. Most of these alerts are self-explanatory, however <b>more details are provided in the Alerts panel.</b>
EVT2742 TEXT x.LogsFrame.TextLabel This monitor displays information regarding logs about both the facility and the reactor. Should something happen that you are <b>uncertain about</b> that you wish to know quickly, <b>looking at the system logs typically helps.</b>
EVT2743 TEXT x.ForecastFrame.TextLabel This monitor displays information about future <b>Subspace Energy Fluctuations</b> that may occur. The most notable fluctuations are known as <u><b>EFEs</b></u>, or <u><b>Equinox.</b></u>
EVT2744 TEXT x.MonitorsFrame.ForecastFrame.TextLabel EFEs or <b>Excessive Energy Fluctuation Events</b> are events which occur when a subspace energy fluctuation <b>exceeds the Haden Threshold</b>. When an EFE is about to occur, all monitors will display a warning.
EVT2745 TEXT x.MainFrame.MonitorsFrame.ForecastFrame.TextLabel <b>Equinox</b> is <b>similar to an EFE,</b> but <b><u>far worse in magnitude, and indefinite in length.</u></b> Their disruptive effects tend to cause massive damage to installation infastructure and should subsequently be avoided at all costs.
EVT2746 TEXT x.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.MonitorsFrame.ForecastFrame.TextLabel While the nature of EFEs and their exact cause remains a mystery, you should <b>refer to the [CORE] panel</b> to learn more about their consequences.
EVT2747 TEXT x.MainframeFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel Overview - The 'Tesseract' <b>Quantum Mainframe</b> is the world's most powerful Quantum Supercomputer, responsible for <b>maintaining</b> extremely complex machinery such as the: <b>CBLs, Reactor Console Command Interface, Gateway Transporters, Medical Dispensers,</b> and more.
EVT2748 TEXT x.MainFrame.MainframeFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel When a <b>QPU fails completely,</b> it will cause connected systems to reconnect and adjust their operations to the remaining QPUs which often results in <b>devices enacting a soft-reboot protocol</b> or in the case of the <b>control room monitors, experience a blue visual distortion.</b>
EVT2749 TEXT x.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.MainframeFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel To <b>replace a QPU</b>, operators will first have to disconnect any remaining connections it has to the mainframe. This can be done by <b>disabling any red indicators on a QPU receptacle.</b> Once the <b>old QPU</b> is <b>extracted</b>, a <b>new one</b> can be <b>inserted</b>, and integration will happen automatically.
EVT2750 TEXT x.DRMScreen.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.MainframeFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel Additionally, components of the <b>mainframe may catch on fire</b> from reactor operation. It's advised that when operators are preparing to replace a QPU, they <b>equip a fire extinguisher.</b> Excessive <b>fires</b> may raise the mainframe temperature and <b>cause QPUs to degrade at a much faster rate.</b>
EVT2751 TEXT x.MainframeFrame.OperationsFrame.TLDRLabel TLDR: If all QPUs degrade fully, the gateway transporters will remain offline until one is replaced. Be aware of mainframe fires, as if theres enough, they can trigger a mainframe meltdown which will cause QPUs to degrade faster.
EVT2752 TEXT x.ElectricGridConsole.DRMScreen.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.MainframeFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel Majority of its computational operations are controlled by <b>QPUs</b>, which have a tendency to <b>degrade</b>, especially in unfavorable environmental conditions or overclocking scenarios.
EVT2753 TEXT x.Workspace.Consoles.ElectricGridConsole.DRMScreen.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.MainframeFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel <b>Gateway Transporters, Medical dispensers, and especially the control room consoles will be non-functional</b> if there are too few QPUs or the mainframe completely shuts down.
EVT2754 TEXT x.Workspace.Consoles.ElectricGridConsole.DRMScreen.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.MainframeFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel Large stockpiles of QPUs tend to be counterintuitive, so operators <b>may have to use a Synthesiser Unit</b> in order to sucessfully replace dying QPUs. Be weary that the mainframe is always kept at extremely low temperatures, and as such haste is of the essence. {wip system}
EVT2755 TEXT x.Workspace.Consoles.ElectricGridConsole.DRMScreen.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.MainframeFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel Operators are required to <b>replace</b> the affected QPU <b>as soon as possible</b>, otherwise machines across the facility will begin to shut off in order to conserve resources.
EVT2756 TEXT x.MainFrame.MainConsFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel <b>Atmosphere Ventilation</b> is a less critical but still vital component of the reactor chamber. These are the vents that line the chamber walls, and are responsible for <b>intaking</b> a <b>large amount of pressure</b> in order to keep the reactor stable.
EVT2757 TEXT x.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.MainConsFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel Overview - The <b>Main Console contains</b> important functions such as emergency systems like the <b>E-Vents and Atmosphere Ventilation</b>, as well as start-up Prerequisites like the <b>Monitor Boot</b> and <b>Master Switch.</b>
EVT2758 TEXT x.MainConsFrame.OperationsFrame.TLDRLabel TLDR: This console controls E-Vents and atmosphere ventilation, as well as major start-up pre-requisites.
EVT2759 TEXT x.PrototypesFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel All upgrades displayed on the reactor operations board have descriptions for what they do, as well as potential side-effects, so further explanation is unecessary. Do note that if your <b>funding is in the negative, you will not be able to purchase any upgrades.</b>
EVT2760 TEXT x.MainFrame.PrototypesFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel Overview - R&D projects created in the Icarus Installation, or other SM Installations are generally refered to as <b>Facility Prototypes.</b> Some of these project developed are able to <b>relieve stress on Reactor Operators.</b>
EVT2761 TEXT x.PrototypesFrame.OperationsFrame.TLDRLabel TLDR: Prototypes are used to make reactor operations easier, though they can only be purchased with any *AVAILABLE* funds. It is in your best interest to not use destructive tactics during reactor operation.
EVT2762 TEXT x.METUFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel Overview - The <b>METU</b> or Mass Electron Transmission Unit is a large structure that sits directly above the subspace core, and is responsible for <b>protecting the facility against a full meltdown.</b>
EVT2763 TEXT x.MainFrame.METUFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel It is powered by <b>two ECC</b>'s (Electron Containment Cell), which are to be <b>replaced</b> once a cell has been <b>expended after firing.</b> ECC's can be replaced by going to the ECC Module in the <b>midsection of the METU</b>, and inserting a cell into the appropriate receptacle. 
EVT2764 TEXT x.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.METUFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel Operators inside of the reactor chamber should <b>avoid maneuvering too far from the core</b>, as it's nullifying <b>gravitational effect</b> has a relatively <b>short range</b>, and exiting this range could cause you to fall and face a lethal injury, even with a Maintenance Exosuit.
EVT2765 TEXT x.METUFrame.OperationsFrame.TLDRLabel TLDR: The METU protects the facility from a full meltdown, however an ECC is expended each time it does so. If both ECC's are depleted, then billions of dollars in damage will occur. ECCs can be replaced by going into the reactor chamber.
EVT2766 TEXT x.ElectricGridConsole.DRMScreen.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.LaserConsFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel <b>Pressure purges</b> should be <b>enacted frequently</b> when necessary, as <b>excessive</b> CBL <b>Pressure</b> can lead to <b>excessive</b> CBL <b>Stress,</b> which can eventually <b>cause</b> a CBL to <b>Overload</b>, which will be fined accordingly.
EVT2767 TEXT x.Workspace.Consoles.ElectricGridConsole.DRMScreen.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.LaserConsFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel This can also be used as another extreme method of cooling the core, however this requires a high degree of precision, especially in higher states, and is very frowned upon by maintenance teams.
EVT2768 TEXT x.Workspace.Consoles.ElectricGridConsole.DRMScreen.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.LaserConsFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel <b>Integrity loss</b> will be <b>displayed in purple</b> for the affected CBL, and will require operators to <b>venture inside of the reactor chamber via M.E.S, and repair the hull breaches within a timely manner.</b>
EVT2769 TEXT x.Workspace.Consoles.ElectricGridConsole.DRMScreen.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.LaserConsFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel Allowing the hull breaches to remain for a <b>prolonged period of time</b> will compromise the affected CBL's reaction sequence, and cause it to <b>gain a substantial increase in pressure</b> until repaired or an overload occurs.
EVT2770 ANIM Workspace.Consoles.ElectricGridConsole.DRMScreen.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.LaserConsFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel moved 5 times inside one poll; dropped as an animation
EVT2771 TEXT x.HDEFFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel Overview - The High Density Electron Field, <b>H.D.E.F,</b> or H-DEF, is responsible for <b>shielding the control froom from hazardous radiation,</b> energy, or even projectiles.
EVT2772 TEXT x.MainFrame.HDEFFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel When the reactor is in a <b>higher energy state</b> than normal, it will typically produce an <b>excessive</b> amount of <b>radiation,</b> which will begin to overwhelm the traditional emitters for the H.D.E.F Shield, resulting in <b>Integrity loss.</b>
EVT2773 TEXT x.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.HDEFFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel Using both the <b>E-Power Supply</b> as well as the <b>M.A.S.S Rails</b> can help <b>resecure</b> the control room from a <b>radiation breach</b> quickly.
EVT2774 TEXT x.HDEFFrame.OperationsFrame.TLDRLabel TLDR: The H.D.E.F Shields the control room from radiation damage, however it's integrity will degrade at a constant rate with excessive radiation or pressure. Enabling the E-Power supply will increase the integrity, however it overheats during reactor operation.
EVT2775 TEXT x.DRMScreen.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.HDEFFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel Activating the <b>E-Power Supply</b> will allow the field to <b>regain integrity</b>, but do note that during reactor operation, it has a <b>tendency to overheat</b>, necessitating it be <b>shut off</b> after a certain amount of time.
EVT2776 TEXT x.ElectricGridConsole.DRMScreen.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.HDEFFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel There are two large emitters beside the control room, as well as an <b>emergency generator behind this console</b> that maintain the field's integrity.
EVT2777 TEXT x.GridConsFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel The <b>Extraction Rate lever</b> controls what <b>percentage of energy</b> is <b>extracted from</b> the <b>Net Output</b> from the core. The <b>total amount of energy extracted</b> can be <b>observed</b> via the <b>Quota Monitoring</b> Screen.
EVT2778 TEXT x.MainFrame.GridConsFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel <b>Stress</b> can be <b>gained</b> by: <b>high extraction rate, low coolant power level, or high temperatures.</b> Enabling and boosting the coolant, or lowering the temperature / extraction rate can help reduce stress.
EVT2779 TEXT x.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.GridConsFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel Overview - The <b>Electric Grid Console</b> is primarily used to manage the <b>P.E.A</b>, and subsequently the <b>quota,</b> however it also controls the <b>Gravatron unit</b> present in Heavy Maintenance.
EVT2780 TEXT x.MainFrame.GridConsFrame.OperationsFrame.TLDRLabel <br />The P.E.A is used to extract energy and achieve the quota, however on higher extration rates or lower coolant levels it will overheat. <br />The gravatron is used to eliminate gravitational wakes, however it's overload mechanism operates on a slim time window.
EVT2781 TEXT x.GravFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel Overview - The <b>Gravatron</b> is a large centrifugal machine that provides power to <b>Grav-Shafts,</b> Grav-Gates, Synthesiser Units, and more via Gravitation Conduits. It's <b>secondary function</b> is to <b>eliminate any gravitation wakes</b> that may appear during reactor operation.
EVT2782 TEXT x.MainFrame.GravFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel Should <b>gravitation wakes</b> from as a result of frequent reactor operation, Operators are recommended to <b>initiate an overload procedure.</b>
EVT2783 TEXT x.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.GravFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel <b>Sucessful overloads</b> can be accomplished by <b>lowering the Grav Output lever to the Overcharge position, and correctly time when to press the Grav Overload button.</b> When the button <b>flashes yellow rapidly</b>, that is when the overcharge is <b>ready to be fired.</b>
EVT2784 TEXT x.GravFrame.OperationsFrame.TLDRLabel Set the Grav Output lever to overcharge, and press the Grav Overload button when it *FLASHES* yellow *RAPIDLY*. If missfired, damage will occur to the gravatron which can cause it to shutdown completely if not repaired.
EVT2785 TEXT x.DRMScreen.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.GravFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel While <b>overcharges normally damage the gravatron</b>, <b>unsuccessful overloads</b> will result in <b>substantially more damage</b>, however this can be repaired with a repair gun.
EVT2786 TEXT x.ElectricGridConsole.DRMScreen.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.GravFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel Excessive damage may result in the Gravatron completely shutting down and disabling certain grav-shafts, requiring they be temporarily powered with GCCs (Gravitation Containment Cells). 
EVT2787 TEXT x.MainFrame.GravFrame.OperationsFrame.TLDRLabel TLDR: The gravatron is used to supply power to the grav-shafts, and its auxillary function is to eliminate gravitational anomalies. 
EVT2788 TEXT x.MaintenanceFrame.TextLabel Overview - In order to assist you with either maintaining degrading equipment, or allow for improved mobility, you're authorized to use several different pieces of equipment which can be <b>acessed</b> via the <b>Maintenace Facilities or Synthesiser Units.</b>
EVT2789 TEXT x.RepairGunFrame.TextLabel A Molecular Weaver or Repair Gun Tool is used to quickly repair minor to moderate damage by mending surrounding materials together while maintaining its overall structual integrity.
EVT2790 TEXT x.EquipmentFrame.RepairGunFrame.TextLabel To repair damage, operators will have to aim and fire the beam directly at the target, and hold for a certain amount of time before stopping.
EVT2791 TEXT x.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.EquipmentFrame.RepairGunFrame.TextLabel A small screen attached to repair guns can help operators guage how close a gun is to overloading, though the B-Grade A-Grade Repair Guns are equipped with better sensors.
EVT2792 TEXT x.MiscellaneousFrame.TextLabel Fire extinguishers operate similarly to Repair Guns with the added benefit of not blowing up in your face. Aim directly at the source of the fire for a few seconds to extinguish it properly.
EVT2793 TEXT x.EquipmentFrame.MiscellaneousFrame.TextLabel QPUs, ECCs, and GCCs are all tools that are inserted into their respective components in order to repair or supply them. More detailed descriptions are available via the Synthesiser Units.
EVT2794 TEXT x.Maintenance Exosuit.TextLabel The Maintenance Exosuit MKII or <b>M.E.S</b> is a heavy duty <b>multi-purpose suit</b> used to operate in high stress environments for maximum efficiency and safety.
EVT2795 TEXT x.EquipmentFrame.Maintenance Exosuit.TextLabel The MkII subvariant that reactor operations personnel will use is designed to <b>withstand high temperatures, extreme radiological environments, and <u>toxic atmospheres</u></b>, making it suitable for repairs inside the chamber during reactor operation.
EVT2796 TEXT x.MainFrame.EquipmentFrame.Maintenance Exosuit.TextLabel It is equipped with a B-Grade Molecular Weaver attachment which acts as a compromise between A-Grade and C-Grade Repair Guns while having <b>substantially more range</b>.
EVT2797 TEXT x.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.EquipmentFrame.Maintenance Exosuit.TextLabel The suit also makes use of an ionic thruster system which PTMs derrive from, making fuel consumption negligible. Unlike PTMs however, this will <b>not dampen high falls.</b>
EVT2798 TEXT x.SubspaceFrame.TextLabel The "Core" of the reactor to put it simply is a wormhole or gateway into Subspace from which energy can be extracted via the P.E.A. It is perhaps the greatest discovery Synthesis Manufacturing and by extension mankind as ever made, and is why it is one of our most top priorties.
EVT2799 TEXT x.CoreFrame.SubspaceFrame.TextLabel Due to the volatility of this core, <b>equipment generally degrades extremely fast</b> in comparison to traditional reactor systems. <b>P.E.A Stress, Chamber Pressure, Radiation, and CBLs are all influenced by the core's temperature</b> with a <b>higher temperature</b> causing more <b>disasterous consequences.</b>
EVT2800 TEXT x.MainFrame.CoreFrame.SubspaceFrame.TextLabel When operating the reactor, <b>you must be vigilant</b> of both the core's temperature, the health of the reactor's major components, the time it takes to achive the quota, and especially your own health in order <b>to succeed at this job.</b>
EVT2801 TEXT x.StatesFrame.State0Label.TextLabel <b>Unresponsive CBLs</b> in this state is indicative of <b>insufficent pressure</b>, and may result in stallout.
EVT2802 TEXT x.CoreFrame.StatesFrame.State0Label.TextLabel If CBL's are unreliable in this state, focus on increasing pressure.
EVT2803 TEXT x.StatesFrame.State1Label.TextLabel Do not underestimate the reactor in this state. It can and will destabilize if you are not careful!
EVT2804 TEXT x.StatesFrame.State2Label.TextLabel Its higher energy output has greater consequences however, as equipment will begin to experience advanced degredation. The <b>H.D.E.F will begin to deteriorate</b> due to excessive radiation, the <b>pressure will increase at a greater rate,</b> and the <b>P.E.A will also gain stress faster.</b>
EVT2805 TEXT x.CoreFrame.StatesFrame.State2Label.TextLabel Excersie caution, and remain vigilant of the reactor. Do not take any unnecessary risk!
EVT2806 TEXT x.StatesFrame.State3Label.TextLabel Due to the extreme instability of the core, operators are required to <b>activate an E-VENT,</b> especially as coolant systems have a tendency of failing at this point.
EVT2807 TEXT x.CoreFrame.StatesFrame.State3Label.TextLabel While the core will produce the most energy in this state, do not hesistate to use an E-VENT to prevent a possible meltdown.
EVT2808 TEXT x.StatesFrame.EFELabel EXCESSIVE ENERGY FLUCTUATION [EFE]
EVT2809 TEXT x.EFELabel.TextLabel Energy Fluctuation Events <b>[EFEs]</b> or Excessive Energy Fluctuation [EEF] <b>occur within a specific time period</b> that is <b>predicted</b> via the <b>Subspace Forecast</b> monitor. Both EFEs and Equinox Events are displayed as a yellow bar, however equinox occurs last.
EVT2810 TEXT x.StatesFrame.EFELabel.TextLabel <b>During an EFE</b>, the core will begin to produce <b>twice as much energy</b> as it normally would, and have minimal temperature fluctuation. Additionally, an <b>EFE will typically create a cross-dimensional anomaly.</b> The anomalies classified thusfar are known as <b>'Thunderquakes'</b>, and <b>'Gravquakes'</b>.
EVT2811 TEXT x.CoreFrame.StatesFrame.EFELabel.TextLabel A <b>'Thunderquake'</b> causes a disruptive electrical spike akin to an EMP that will <b>temporarily disable control room functionality</b> among several other subsystems. The electrical shortages caused by this will <b> damage the facility mainframe.</b>
EVT2812 TEXT x.MainFrame.CoreFrame.StatesFrame.EFELabel.TextLabel A <b>'Gravquake'</b> will cause a large portion of the installation to <b>experience microgravity.</b> Additionally, the <b>gravatron will suffer damage</b>, though the exact cause is unknown outside of an energy spike in gravitation conduits.
EVT2813 TEXT x.StatesFrame.EquinoxLabel EQUINOX STATE [TEMPORAL]
EVT2814 TEXT x.EquinoxLabel.TextLabel The Equinox State or Equinox Event is a massive spike in normal energy fluctuations that causes a disruptive effect to electronic equipment, as well as destabilization within the core.
EVT2815 TEXT x.CoreFrame.StatesFrame.EquinoxLabel.TextLabel While the Equinox State has the <b>potential</b> of producing a <b>large amount of power</b>, allowing the quota to be achived quicker, its <b>high instability</b> combined with its disruptive effects makes this an <b>extremely risky</b> operation.
EVT2816 TEXT x.MainFrame.CoreFrame.StatesFrame.EquinoxLabel.TextLabel Pressure control systems will become inoperable, CBLs will gain pressure much faster, the Coolant will experience much faster degredation, with the potential of rupturing completely, and many more unforseen consequences. 
EVT2817 TEXT x.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.CoreFrame.StatesFrame.EquinoxLabel.TextLabel <u>Shut down the reactor as soon as possible</u> if an Equinox State is achieved.
EVT2818 TEXT x.DRMScreen.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.CoreFrame.StatesFrame.EquinoxLabel.TextLabel This is byfar the most mysterious phenomena of the subspace core, however further research has been suspended due to the unacceptable amount of damage it causes to company infastructure.
EVT2819 TEXT x.StartupFrame.TextLabel MONITOR POWER > [ALT REACTOR CONSOLE]
EVT2820 TEXT x.BasicsFrame.StartupFrame.TextLabel CONTROL ROOM SHUTTERS > [ALT REACTOR CONSOLE]
EVT2821 TEXT x.MainFrame.BasicsFrame.StartupFrame.TextLabel MONITOR BOOT > [MAIN REACTOR CONSOLE]
EVT2822 TEXT x.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.BasicsFrame.StartupFrame.TextLabel NOTICE: ACTIVATE THE HDEF GENERATOR TO PREVENT POTENTIAL CONTROL ROOM RADIATION BREACH DURING START-UP. BE ADVISED THAT DURING REACTOR OPERATION, THE HDEF GENERATOR CAN OVERHEAT. PERIODICALLY SHUT IT OFF TO PREVENT OVERLOAD.
EVT2823 TEXT x.DRMScreen.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.BasicsFrame.StartupFrame.TextLabel FLIP MASTER SWITCH TO START-UP POSITION
EVT2824 TEXT x.MainFrame.BasicsFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel As reactor operations personnel, you are required to <b>Achieve the target energy quota, and sucessfully shut down the reactor each shift.</b> You will be nickel and dimed for any company property damaged or destroyed in any way. <b>Remaining funds</b> can be used to <b>invest in equipment</b> to make your job easier.
EVT2825 TEXT x.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.BasicsFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel Sucessfully completed shifts will reward you with <b>greater access to the facility</b> and potential for monetary benefits, however if the <b>debt ceiling is reached, disciplinary action will occur.</b> Good luck! The future of industry starts with you!
EVT2826 TEXT x.AltConsFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel <b>Control Room Functions</b> are primairily to <b>fufill start-up prerequisites</b>, or for your personal comfort. Generally this part of the console can be completely ignored once eveything is set up.
EVT2827 TEXT x.MainFrame.AltConsFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel Overview - The <b>Alt Reactor Console</b> is for managing auxillary functions to the reactor such as the <b>M.A.S.S Rails</b>, as well as some <b>control room functions.</b>
EVT2828 TEXT x.AltConsFrame.OperationsFrame.TLDRLabel TLDR: This console controls certain start-up prerequisites as well as the M.A.S.S Rails. <br />M.A.S.S Rails act as radiation scrubbers which can help protect the control room from radiation damage, however it will reduce the amount of power that can be extracted.
EVT2829 TEXT x.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.AltConsFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel These are to be activated in tandem with the <b>H.D.E.F</b> to help prevent or <b>eliminate a radiation breach.</b> Note that the <b>M.A.S.S Rails</b> will <b>interfere</b> with the core's <b>energy output</b>, and <b>lower</b> the amount of net <b>Energy</b> that can be <b>extracted,</b> though this can be mitgated with a lower M.A.S.S Power Level.
EVT2830 TEXT x.DRMScreen.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.AltConsFrame.OperationsFrame.TextLabel The <b>M.A.S.S Rails</b> are a massively upscaled variant of the scrubbers found in deionization chambers which help protect vital machinery and personnel from chamber emissions.
EVT2831 TEXT x.GeneralFrame.TextLabel These are the alerts that are by the top and are generally unimportant, with the most notable being <b>Chamber Radiation & Reactor Activation</b> warnings.
EVT2832 TEXT x.AlertsFrame.GeneralFrame.TextLabel Vacate all personnel from the chamber immediately during reactor activation, there are numerous mechanical hazards, as well as the extremely lethal burst of energy during the ignition or even subspace generator activation phases.
EVT2833 TEXT x.MainFrame.AlertsFrame.GeneralFrame.TextLabel Remember to keep the H.D.E.F. E-Generator (behind you) active during the start-up sequence. The large burst in energy during ignition typically degrades the field to the point of causing radiation damage.
EVT2834 TEXT x.ModerateFrame.TextLabel These Alerts provide general information about both the core and the control room, and should <b>divert your attention</b> but <b>not</b> be the <b>biggest priority.</b> The most notable alert would be <b>Radiation Breach.</b>
EVT2835 TEXT x.AlertsFrame.ModerateFrame.TextLabel A <b>Radiation breach</b> is a sign of <b>low H.D.E.F Integrity.</b> You will be fined accordingly for any damage caused by radiation, as well as accompanying medical expenses. <b>Activating</b> the H.D.E.F <b>E-Power supply</b> should help <b>resolve a radiation breach.</b>
EVT2836 TEXT x.MajorFrame.TextLabel These alerts provides priority information about the reactor, and are often the alerts that you should be <b>looking out for the most.</b>
EVT2837 TEXT x.AlertsFrame.MajorFrame.TextLabel <b>Excessive Temperature</b> is a relatively passive alert that <b>signals advanced degredation.</b>
EVT2838 TEXT x.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.AlertsFrame.MajorFrame.TextLabel <b>Energy Fluctuation</b> (Excessive Energy Fluctuation Event or EFE) will usually occur at <b>specific times</b> that are <b>predicted</b> via the <b>Subpace Forecast Monitor.</b> More details are provided in the Core panel.
EVT2839 TEXT x.ElectricGridConsole.DRMScreen.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.AlertsFrame.MajorFrame.TextLabel <b>Hazardous Chamber Radiation</b> and <b>High Chamber Pressure</b> will both <b>decrease</b> the <b>H.D.E.F's integrity</b>, however High Chamber Pressure will also cause an <b>increase</b> in <b>Temp Fluctuation.</b>
EVT2840 TEXT x.Workspace.Consoles.ElectricGridConsole.DRMScreen.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.AlertsFrame.MajorFrame.TextLabel <b>High CBL Power</b> will cause the <b>CBL pressure</b> & subsequently the <b>stress</b> to rise dramatically, and <b>High CBL Stress</b> is a sign that a <b>pressure purge is needed</b> to avoid imminent overload.
EVT2841 TEXT x.MalfunctionsFrame.TextLabel These Alerts signal if <b>vital components</b> of the reactor are <b>offline, damaged, or even destroyed</b>. It will also signal <b>special core circumstances</b> such as <b>stallout.</b>
EVT2842 TEXT x.AlertsFrame.MalfunctionsFrame.TextLabel <b>P.E.A Malfunction</b> means that the <b>Power Extraction Assembly</b> is currently <b>offline,</b> typically <b>from excessive stress.</b> It will automatically shut itself off to prevent an explosion, and will <b>take a while to reboot.</b>
EVT2843 TEXT x.MainFrame.AlertsFrame.MalfunctionsFrame.TextLabel <b>Pump Malfunction & Low Coolant Reservior</b> is a sign that the <b>C-Pumps are offline </b>and in need of repairs. Operators are required to <b>go into Reactor Coolant Maintenance and fix the issue.</b>
EVT2844 TEXT x.SurfaceGui.MainFrame.AlertsFrame.MalfunctionsFrame.TextLabel <b>CBL Malfunction</b> signals at a <b>major problem</b> with a CBL. During reactor operation, as <b>CBL's</b> are <b>integral to maintaining the core</b>, they should be <b>active at all times.</b> This warning will go off <b>while a CBL is currently disabled</b>, or if the <b>reactor pressure is too low.</b>
